{"id":"920a0c15-0afe-493b-8fff-a324ffaf3e52","object":"chat.completion","created":1785030456,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    min_sum = sum(seg['minimum'] for seg in segments)\\n    cap_sum = sum(seg['capacity'] for seg in segments)\\n    if budget < min_sum or budget > cap_sum:\\n        raise ValueError('Infeasible budget')\\n    alloc = [seg['minimum'] for seg in segments]\\n    remaining = budget - min_sum\\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_score = -1.0\\n        for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\\n            if alloc[i] < seg['capacity']:\\n                score = seg['importance'] / (alloc[i] + 1)\\n                if score > best_score:\\n                    best_score = score\\n                    best_idx = i\\n        if best_idx == -1:\\n            break\\n        alloc[best_idx] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    by_name = {segments[i]['name']: alloc[i] for i in range(n)}\\n    total = sum(alloc)\\n    return {'allocations': alloc, 'by_name': by_name, 'total': total}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that implements the described algorithm. The case payload structure is: `segments` (list of dicts with name, importance, minimum, capacity), `budget`. The algorithm:\n\n- Initialize each segment at its minimum quota.\n- Check if budget < sum(minimums) -> raise ValueError; also if budget > sum(capacities) -> raise ValueError.\n- Then while remaining budget > 0, allocate one token to the segment that maximizes (importance / (current allocation + 1))? Actually description: \"give one token to the segment maximizing importance divided by its next allocated position.\" That means for each segment, after current allocation `alloc_i`, the next token would be at position `alloc_i + 1` (if we consider 1-indexed). The value = importance / (alloc_i + 1). But careful: \"importance divided by its next allocated position\". If current allocation is 0, next position is 1 => importance/1. That matches typical proportional allocation. But we must use floating division. In case of ties, use original segment order (stable tie break). So we need to pick the segment with maximum value, breaking ties by order in the list.\n\nBut also note: segments have capacities. Must not exceed capacity. So before allocating to a segment, check if current allocation < capacity.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n- Compute total_min = sum(seg['minimum'] for seg in case['segments'])\n- total_capacity = sum(seg['capacity'] for seg in case['segments'])\n- budget = case['budget']\n- if budget < total_min or budget > total_capacity: raise ValueError\n- Initialize allocations = [seg['minimum'] for seg in case['segments']]\n- remaining = budget - total_min\n- While remaining > 0:\n  - For each i, if allocations[i] < case['segments'][i]['capacity'], compute score = importance / (allocations[i] + 1). (or maybe importance / (allocations[i] + 1) because next position is current+1? Actually \"next allocated position\" if we allocate 0 initially, next would be first? But we already gave minimum; so after minimum, next position for additional token is minimum+1? Let's parse: \"Initialize every segment at its minimum quota. While budget remains, give one token to the segment maximizing importance divided by its next allocated position.\" The next allocated position after current allocation means if we have allocated x tokens, the next token will be the (x+1)-th token. So value = importance / (x+1). That's typical for proportional allocation with initial minimums.\n  - Find index with max score; break ties by smallest index (original order).\n  - Increment allocations[i], decrement remaining.\n- After loop, compute total = sum(allocations) (should equal budget).\n- Compute by_name dict: key = segment name, value = allocation.\n- Return {'allocations': allocations, 'by_name': by_name, 'total': total}\n\nEdge cases: Ensure integer scores? The division yields float; use float comparison. But for tie-breaking, if scores are equal, pick earlier segment.\n\nAlso note: The schema requires `by_name` object keys from `case.segments[*].name`. So we must use those names exactly.\n\nThe function must not mutate case; it's safe to read only.\n\nImplementation should be in Python standard library.\n\nWe need to output JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a string.\n\nLet's write the code. Also include docstring and comments? Not required but fine.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1499,"completion_tokens":1050,"total_tokens":2549,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":761},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":91},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}